This is an article about a certain idea for implementing the [holy grail](/grimoire/grail-1).


The core concept is that we blend Vale's region handling with Nick's group borrowing concept.


Remember from the holy grail article, that the original goal is to support a program like this:


```
func continueChase(level: Level, entity: Entity) {
  // Run-time overhead here to get an exclusive lock
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    // Get borrow reference to contained ChaseTarget
    case .some(let chaseTarget):
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}
```


...but without the overhead.



To do that, we'll:

 * Start with Group Borrowing.
 * Add many-typed groups.
 * Introduce an implicit many-typed group.
 * Introduce a refcounted group, make ref drops invalidate references into it.
 * Generalize mutations to full effects.
 * Add the ability for "defensive" mutably-aliasing group annotations, similar to Vale's readonly groups.
 * Add intra-object isolation.
 * Add anchors.


Let's dive in!


# Start with Group Borrowing

First, let's phrase the snippet in terms of group borrowing, with no reference counting.

```
fn continueChase(mut level: ref Level, mut entity: ref level.entities.* Entity):
  match entity.chaseTarget:
    case Some(ref chaseTarget):
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let mut ref tile = level.tiles[entity.loc]:
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
      break
    case None:
      break

struct Level:
  ...
  fn harvestNatureEnergy(self: Level, mut tile: ref level.tiles.* Tile, entity: Entity):
    ...
```

Some things to note first:

 * This is a de-sugared, expanded version.
 * `mut level: ref Level` means we might modify anything inside Level.
 * `mut entity: ref level.entities.* Entity` means `entity` is a reference to an Entity inside the level.entities list.
 * In `fn harvestNatureEnergy(self: Level, mut tile: Tile, entity: Entity):`, notice only `tile` has a `mut` on it. That means that we'll modify the contents of the tile and nothing else.


I'll use a more Swift-like syntax, since it's more familiar, and its reference counting will come into play later:


```
func continueChase(
    mut level: Level,
    mut entity: level.entities.* Entity
) {
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    case .some(let chaseTarget): // Borrow ref
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}

struct Level {
  ...
  func harvestNatureEnergy(
      self: Level,
      tile: level.tiles.* Tile mut,
      entity: Entity
  ) {
    ...
  }
}
```

Note that everything is a struct, to match the semantics of group borrowing.


# Add many-typed groups

Now, we're going to do a certain transformation to our program.


The goal is to remove the `level.entities.*` and `level.tiles.*` annotations from the above.

To do that, we'll take one step back and then two steps forward. It's going to look more complex for a bit, and then we'll reduce it afterward.


We're going to "relationalize" it. We'll make it more like a relational database.


Instead of this hierarchy:

(insert pic)


We'll flatten our objects into a bunch of central array lists in a "Multi" class:

(insert pic)

This might sound familiar: this is from Arrrlang!

Note that this Multi class will disappear soon. It's just a stepping stone to aid in explaining.


Now our snippet looks like this:


```
func continueChase(
    multi: Multi,
    level: multi.levels.* Level mut,
    entity: multi.entities.* Entity mut
) {
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    case .some(let chaseTarget): // Borrow ref
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}

struct Level {
  ...
  func harvestNatureEnergy(
      multi: Multi,
      self: multi.levels.* Level,
      tile: multi.tiles.* Tile mut,
      entity: multi.entities.* Entity
  ) {
    ...
  }
}
```


Everything is still a struct.


This Multi is really just a struct that owns a lot of arenas. It's rather expensive (it has an arena for every single type) but it will go away soon.


Let's call its interior a "many-typed group", since it owns many groups, each with a different type.


# Ownership

When we let go of an owning pointer, there's only one thing that happens: it signals a mutation to `multi.whatever`. (NOT `multi.whatever.*`.)

This will indirectly invalidate any reference to any Whatever owned by the Multi.


This is the key that makes this all work. this is how we invalidate all extant Whatever borrow refs whenever we drop any Whatever.


(need expanded example that shows a reference being invalidated)


# Multi is just a Heap

Let's be honest. Multi is really just a heap! Or rather, it's a per-type heap.


What happens if we just get rid of it and replace it with malloc/free?


It works!


But keep in mind that we still communicate paths as if there is a Multi. The Multi lives on in spirit.

The Multi is no more, but the multi-typed group is still here.


```
func continueChase(
    // Implicit multi: Multi,
    level: Level mut, // Implicit multi.levels.*
    entity: Entity mut // Implicit multi.entities.*
) {
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    case .some(let chaseTarget): // Borrow ref
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}

struct Level {
  ...
  func harvestNatureEnergy(
      // Implicit multi: Multi,
      self: Level, // Implicit multi.levels.* 
      tile: Tile mut, // Implicit multi.tiles.* 
      entity: Entity // Implicit multi.entities.* 
  ) {
    ...
  }
}
```


# Introduce reference counting [#gengrail]


Now, we'll introduce a `rc` keyword.


Whenever an `rc` goes out of scope, we'll invalidate all references to that group, exactly like our owning reference did.

After all, it might have just been deleted.


We'll relax this later with anchors.



(need expanded example that shows a reference being invalidated)


```
func continueChase(
    level: rc Level mut,
    entity: rc Entity mut
) {
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    case .some(let chaseTarget): // Borrow ref
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}

struct Level {
  ...
  func harvestNatureEnergy(
      self: Level,
      tile: Tile mut,
      entity: Entity
  ) {
    ...
  }
}
```


# Add some sweetness

When using rc, this will be common:

```
func continueChase(
    level: rc Level mut,
    entity: rc Entity mut
) {
```

and in fact, we'll often want to modify things that aren't in the parameter list, like:

```
func continueChase(
    level: rc Level mut,
    entity: rc Entity mut
) mut Tile {
```


So we'll add this sugar that means "we might modify any refcounted thing":


```
mut func continueChase(
    level: rc Level,
    entity: rc Entity
) {
```

I expect this will be particularly common for interface methods.

This does, of course, mean that we can't hold borrow references across these function calls.

Irksome, but let's fix that.


# Defensive aliasing groups

Consider this function (case A):

```
func moveComponent(a: &Spaceship mut, b: &Spaceship) {
  a.system = b.system
}
```

It clears out `a.system` and then copies `b.system` into it. But if these are secretly pointing at the same instance, bad things happen.

Thats why we reject this.

If we assume these alias, then we have a problem.


There are also times when we're totally fine if they overlap, for example (case B):

```
func tradeGoldForWood(
    pull_gold_from_account: &Spaceship mut,
    put_wood_into_account: &Spaceship mut,
    amount: int
) {
  pull_gold_from_account.wood -= amount
  put_wood_into_account.wood += amount
}
```


And sometimes we read from one and write to another (case C):

```
func heal(
    healer: &Entity,
    target: &Entity mut
) {
  target.hp += healer.skill
}
```

(TODO: add a bleeding component that is removed)

So the question arises, what should be the default?

Do we assume all things of the same type alias?

(maybe talk about that)

Or do we assume that all things are mutually isolated?

Let's talk about that case.


It's weird because there might be a caller in case C which is handing in the same, if someone is healing themselves:

```
if Some(injury_ref) = entity.maybe_injury:
  heal(entity, entity)
  print(injury_ref.severity) # Error: `heal` call may have invalidated entity.maybe_injury
```

...which is an accurate error.

However, another case C caller might have handed in different entities:

```
fn player_heals_pet(player: &Entity, pet: &Entity mut):
  if pet.maybe_injury.is_some():
    if Some(player_injury_ref) = player.maybe_injury:
      heal(player, pet)
      print("Your \(player_injury_ref.description) injury interfered, but you healed the pet a little.")
    else:
      heal(player, pet)
      print("You healed the pet a lot.")
```

Here, we don't want to 





need better example... this is an example where we dont want to allow overlapping.






This will be particularly useful for generics.








This means that anything might be modified. I expect this to be pretty common.



Anyone using a class will need to specify the Multi that it comes from. And more importantly, if it's not specified, an implicit default will be added. The below snippet is semantically equivalent to the above:


```
func continueChase(
    // Implicit multi: Multi,
    level: Level mut, // Implicit multi.levels.*
    entity: Entity mut // Implicit multi.entities.*
) {
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    case .some(let chaseTarget): // Borrow ref
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}

struct Level {
  ...
  func harvestNatureEnergy(
      // Implicit multi: Multi,
      self: Level, // Implicit multi.levels.* 
      tile: Tile mut, // Implicit multi.tiles.* 
      entity: Entity // Implicit multi.entities.* 
  ) {
    ...
  }
}
```

Boom! We've accomplished our immediate goal.

However, this `multi: Multi` is still there, and it's rather expensive. It has an array list for every single type. Is there any way to get rid of it?


Yes there is! Let's add some reference counting.


<slice>
#gengrail: We could totally make `class` do generational references. Any class reference is really just a generational index into the implicit Multi region. New blend!
</slice>




# Remove Multi

Now, let's remove the `Multi`.


Now there's no place for these objects to live!


Every `class` is reference counted. It lives on the heap. Every reference into `multi.whatever.*` shares ownership of it.






 `class`es reference counted.



[#rctwist]




<slice>
#rctwist: You could say that there's only ever one owning reference, and all non-owning references are weak references. You can turn a weak reference into a borrow reference as long as nothing deletes any of that type.
</slice>







Before I talk about the mechanisms, I'll set the stage a bit.

Imagine our program had this data:

enum ShipComponent {
    struct RazaEngine { warp_target str; }
    struct FireflyEngine { impulse int; }
}
class Ship {
    fuel int;
    name str;
    crew ArrayList<strString>;
    components ArrayList<ShipComponent>;
}
class LaunchPad {
    ship Ship;
}
class SpaceStation {
    ships ArrayList<Ship>;
    launch_pads ArrayList<LaunchPad>;
}

Notes on syntax:
This is ~Vale-ish syntax (except the enum and class)
enum works similar to Rust.
class and struct both exist. 
struct lives inline in the containing stack, array, struct, or object.
class lives on the heap, and is reference counted.
ArrayList is a struct.
str is a class.

Now imagine that we had this function:

func copy_first_component(
    station SpaceStation,
    from_ship Ship,  // Precondition: has components
    mut to_ship Ship,
) {
    first_component_ref = &from_ship.components[0]
    to_ship.components.append(first_component_ref.copy())
    println("Moved " + get_name(first_component_ref))
}

This isn't memory safe (if from_ship and to_ship are the same, the append call could invalidate our first_component reference), so our approach should catch that and give an error here.

Let's desugar the above and make it more explicit:

enum ShipComponent {
    struct RazaEngine { warp_target str; }
    struct FireflyEngine { impulse int; }
}
class Ship<a'> {
    fuel int;
    name a'str;             // ← Note this new a'
    crew ArrayList<a'str>;  // ← Note this new a'
    components ArrayList<ShipComponent>; // ← No a' here because no classes
}
class LaunchPad<a'> {
    ship a'Ship;  // ← note this new a'
}
class SpaceStation<a'> {
    ships ArrayList<a'Ship>;             // ← Note the a' on Spaceship
                                         //    but not on the ArrayList
    launch_pads ArrayList<a'LaunchPad>;  // ← Same here
}

// Note the a' region parameter, that will be implicitly present for any
// function that receives a class as an argument.
func copy_first_component<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,  // Note the a' here.
    from_ship a'Ship,        // Note the a' here.
    to_ship a'Ship,          // Note the a' here.
                             // These are in the same group!
){mut a'Ship'_contents} {  // Moved effect here (no semantic difference)
    // Type contains the path
    first_component_ref &a'Ship'_contents'components'items' ShipComponent =
        &from_ship.components[0]
    temp = first_component_ref.copy()
    to_ship.components.append(temp)
    println("Moved " + get_name(first_component_ref))
}

Notes on syntax:
a' multi rc declares a group named a' that is:
Multi-typed.
Reference counted.

Let's also look at ArrayList's append method:

fn append<x'>(self x'ArrayList<T>, item T){mut x'} void {
    ...
}

When the caller to_ship.components.append(temp) calls it, the compiler infers that the callee's x' group maps to the caller's a'Ship'_contents'components group.

So what's that  {mut x'} thing? That's a mutation effect.

Using the above mapping, the callsite sees this and knows that a'Ship'_contents'components' has been modified.

It will note this down and continue compiling our copy_first_component function.

The next line in copy_first_component is println("Moved " + get_name(first_component)) and the compiler must ask itself, is it safe to use this reference?

That first_component_ref's group is a'Ship'_contents'components'items'. That's a child-group of something that was modified (a'Ship'_contents'components') and therefore is invalid, the user can't dereference it.

The compiler prints out an error message. BOOM. Victory.



Notes: (todo move these)

Because it's multi-typed, it has many child groups, one per concrete type. It has many "type-child-groups".
Because it's reference counted:
The group actually represents the conceptual collection that has conceptual ownership of these reference-counted objects. You can think of it as the heap, the set of global ArrayList that actually own these objects, which actually deallocates them when their counter gets to zero.
A reference-counted group will have a _contents child group. But here, since it's combined with a multi, each type-child-group has a _contents child group.
Mutations to this group mean that _all_ objects in the group are invalid (not sure if this is even possible so we might not need to worry about it)
Mutations to e.g. a'Spaceship mean that a reference count was incremented/decremented. Mutations to e.g. a'Spaceship'_contents means only that some Spaceship's contents changes.




Why did we move the mut to an effects list?

Makes the model a little more flexible, a function's able to express that it modified only part of something, not the whole thing.

Most of the time hopefully the user can just write it in the argument list like before, but occasionally putting it in the effects list might be necessary.

