Reference Count Decrementing Doesn't Mean Invalidation

We thought that dropping a reference (to a class) meant that we 


// Precondition: station has ships
func destroy_first_ship<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,
) {
    last_ship_ref = station.ships.pop_back()
    // drop last_ship_ref
}

The compiler should complain.
First of all, we're modifying something in station, so we need to have a mut a'SpaceStation'_contents effect.
Second, we're dropping an a'Ship (specifically last_ship_ref) and the callers need to know to invalidate any references into any a'Ships, so we need to have a mut a'Ship effect.

So the correct function would be:

// Precondition: station has ships
func destroy_first_ship<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,
){mut a'SpaceStation'_contents, mut a'Ship} {
    last_ship_ref = station.ships.pop_back()
    // drop last_ship_ref
}

Aside: If the user doesn't want the mut a'Ship effect, then they can return the reference instead:
// Precondition: station has ships
func destroy_first_ship<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,
){mut a'SpaceStation'_contents} a'Ship {  // Returns a'Ship
    last_ship_ref = station.ships.pop_back()
    return last_ship_ref
}
This could be a common pattern in users' code: to avoid a "drop effect", return the reference to the caller. The caller might then also return it to _its_ caller. Or perhaps the caller will stash it away into some data structure to be cleaned up after the current work is done. This almost generalizes to something like autorelease pools (from pre-ARC objective-C). This might be a way to enforce correct usage of that pattern.

Okay back to the version with the effect. This will be inconvenient to some callers:

func my_func<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,
){mut a'Ship'_contents, mut a'Ship} {
    ship_name_ref &a'Ship'_contents'Str = &station.ships[0].name

    destroy_first_ship<a'>(station)

    // Error: Dereferencing invalid reference to a'Ship
    println(ship_name_ref)
}

They get an error because destroy_first_ship told its callers that it modified a'Ship, and we're trying to dereference something in a'Ship'_contents'.

The workaround is pretty simple: the user should store a reference local variable:


func my_func<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,
){mut a'Ship'_contents, mut a'Ship} {
    ship_ref &a'Ship = &station.ships[0]  // increments Ship's rc count

    // Disregard this different path for now, we'll get to that.
    ship_name_ref &ship_ref'_contents'Str = &ship_ref.name

    destroy_first_ship<a'>(station)

    // Not an error!
    println(ship_name_ref)
}

Suddenly the user can keep using their ship_ref and ship_name_ref, because the system ties it to a live reference.

Local References

Here's a similar example that doesn't destroy any ships, but it _does_ destroy a reference.

func my_func<a' multi rc>(
    station a'SpaceStation,
){mut a'Ship'_contents, mut a'Ship} {
    ship_ref &a'Ship = &station.ships[0]  // increments Ship's rc count

    // Since this reference came from a local, that's encoded into this
    // reference's path.
    ship_name_ref &ship_ref'_contents'Str = &ship_ref.name

    println(ship_name_ref)

    // ship_ref goes out of scope, decrementing the Ship's rc count.
    // Since it's a local variable created in this function, it doesn't
    // count as us modifying a'Ship ourselves.
}

For now, we'll increment the RC whenever we hand it to a callee. The callee receiving an RC reference will destroy it on return.

